CNS models were screened as spheroids or on matrix-coated plates (termed ‘coat’ hereafter) and organoids were screened in Matrigel domes or in coat conditions, all in serum-free media (Supplementary Table 1). Gene expression and essentiality profiles were nearly identical across CNS growth formats (Pearson’s r = 0.996 and 0.990, respectively), and organoid gene dependencies also strongly agreed between the two conditions (r = 0.984; Extended Data Fig. 4c–h). These results suggest that growth format does not significantly affect CRISPR screen outcomes under serum-free conditions. This finding is in contrast to a previous study that used serum-containing media22—a discrepancy that may be attributable to differences in medium composition and the comparison strategy used. All models were profiled for WGS, RNA-seq and CRISPR under identical conditions.

Next, we explored vulnerabilities tied to glial gene expression. We identified an enrichment of CDK6 dependency in glial compared with mesenchymal models (Δdependency = –0.44, P = 0.013) (Fig. 3d,e). Although this dependency ranked second in effect size, it fell short of the multiple-testing significance threshold (q = 0.28), which may be due to the small sample size of glial GBMs (n = 36) analysed. Comparisons with other NextGen models confirmed that this enrichment was driven by the glial state rather than serum-free media (Fig. 3e and Extended Data Fig. 6e). Encouraged by the clinical impact of CDK4 and CDK6 (CDK4/6) inhibitors on treatment outcomes, we pursued CDK6 dependency and its associated biomarker.

To better understand the combined dataset of NextGen and traditional models, we studied differences in gene essentiality between NextGen 3D organoids and traditional 2D models. We compared 3D organoids grown in Matrigel domes with serum-free media (containing EGF and FGF10) to lineage-matched 2D adherent models cultured directly on plastic with serum-containing media (referred to as 3D (n = 94) and 2D (n = 433) models, respectively). We maintained consistent culture conditions in each cohort to elucidate the effects of these conditions.

To broadly compare gene essentialities, we calculated the difference in mean dependency scores between 3D and 2D models for every gene (n = 16,691; excluding common essential genes), and identified gene sets (Hallmark and KEGG gene sets from MSigDB; q < 0.05) that were significantly overrepresented at the extremes of this difference (Fig. 5a,b). Clustering these gene sets by component overlap (>0.3; Extended Data Fig. 8h) merged two sets (adhesion and cytoskeleton (adhesion–cytoskeleton), and cell cycle), whereas five others (lipid metabolism, mTORC1, oxidative phosphorylation, UV response and systemic lupus erythematosus) remained separate.Fig. 5Comparing 2D and 3D dependencies to disentangle the effects of culture conditions.a,b, Gene sets enriched for differential dependencies between 3D and 2D models (n = 94 (3D) and 433 (2D)). Odds ratios for the 9 significant gene sets (q < 0.05) overrepresented in the 200 outlier genes were plotted, clustered and coloured by gene overlap (>0.3; a). Mean dependency differences and their significance for individual genes (two-tailed Mann–Whitney U-test; gene n = 3,521), coloured by the gene set clusters from a were also plotted (b). c–e, Minipool screens were performed to elucidate the differing effects of growth format and culture medium. A minipool screen (250 test genes) was designed (c) to separate the effects of growth format (plastic versus dome) and culture medium (RPMI–FBS versus OPAC) on gene essentiality. The screens identified genes for which dependencies are primarily altered by growth format (d) or culture medium (e), with red dashed lines indicating significance threshold (q = 0.05, two-sided linear regression t-test) and the point colours indicating gene classes in the minipool library (bottom). Screen n = 11 (dome), 7 (plastic), 9 (OPAC), 9 (RPMI–FBS). f,g, Comparisons across growth formats and culture media. f, Different gene classes represented in the minipool library are highlighted. Integrin and actin regulation classes exhibited significant variance across growth formats. g, By contrast, lipid metabolism class were affected by culture medium (q < 0.05, two-tailed F-test; gene n = 200). h, Differential effects on integrin and cholesterol homeostasis dependencies assessed by viability after test gene knockout. Values are the mean ± s.d. (n = 3). P values calculated using two-tailed two-way ANOVA. R–F, RPMI–FBS. i, Colocalization of integrin chains (green) with FAK (red) in KP3 cells under plastic or dome growth conditions (both in RPMI–FBS). Nuclei (blue) and actin (white) are also shown. Representative images from three independent experiments are displayed. Scale bar, 20 μm.Source data

